\section{Extended Per-Task Results}
\label{app:per_task}

Table~\ref{tab:per_task} reports per-task mean CAR across seeds for each condition. Seven tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla, while three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6) fall below 0.58. The overall CAR differences reported in the main text are dominated by these three hard tasks, which average 0.342 compared to 0.968 for the seven easy tasks.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-task mean CAR across seeds (50 rollouts per seed, v1 code). This table reports v1 codebase results retained for completeness; all main analyses use v2 data (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). All conditions use 3 seeds except Depth (4). Detach+LN per-task uses 2 seeds (s1, s2; s0 lacks per-task breakdown).}
\label{tab:per_task}
\begin{tabular}{clccccccc}
\toprule
 & & \multicolumn{7}{c}{Mean CAR by Condition} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-9}
Task & Diff. & Vanilla & Full OCC & Random & Detach & Det+LN & Depth & Cut@35K \\
\midrule
T0 & Easy  & 0.98 & 0.98 & 0.99 & 0.97 & 0.97 & 0.99 & 0.98 \\
T1 & Easy  & 0.92 & 0.93 & 0.99 & 0.89 & 0.78 & 0.93 & 0.95 \\
T2 & Easy  & 0.92 & 0.93 & 0.94 & 0.93 & 0.90 & 0.90 & 0.94 \\
T3 & Hard  & 0.38 & 0.37 & 0.31 & \textbf{0.11} & 0.44 & 0.50 & 0.55 \\
T4 & Easy  & 0.98 & 0.99 & 0.95 & 0.98 & 0.96 & 0.92 & 0.97 \\
T5 & Hard  & 0.07 & 0.17 & 0.23 & \textbf{0.05} & 0.12 & 0.08 & 0.04 \\
T6 & Hard  & 0.57 & 0.61 & 0.55 & 0.56 & 0.36 & 0.54 & 0.38 \\
T7 & Easy  & 0.97 & 0.99 & 0.95 & 0.98 & 1.00 & 0.96 & 0.98 \\
T8 & Easy  & 1.00 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 0.99 & 0.99 & 0.99 & 0.99 \\
T9 & Easy  & 1.00 & 0.99 & 0.99 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 1.00 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Overall} & 0.780 & 0.797 & 0.789 & 0.746 & 0.765 & 0.780 & 0.779 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Ceiling effects.} On the seven easy tasks (T0--T2, T4, T7--T9), all conditions achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.89, leaving minimal room for auxiliary training to improve performance. The obstacle is spatially separated from the manipulation path in these tasks.

\textbf{Hard task patterns.} On Task~5 (Vanilla CAR 0.07), gradient-flow conditions (Random OCC 0.23, Full OCC 0.17) exceed Vanilla and Detach (0.05), but all values remain below 0.23. Cutoff@35K achieves the highest T3 CAR (0.55) but the lowest T5 CAR (0.04), which illustrates task-specific temporal sensitivity. Depth exhibits high cross-seed variance on hard tasks (T3 range: 0.20--0.78; T6 range: 0.16--0.84), consistent with auxiliary supervision increasing seed sensitivity.

\section{Training Details}
\label{app:training}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Training hyperparameters.}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
Base model & OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} \\
Fine-tuning recipe & OFT~\cite{kim2025oft} \\
Vision encoders & DINOv2 (1024-d) + SigLIP (1152-d) \\
Patch token dim. & 2176 (concatenated) \\
LoRA rank / $\alpha$ & 32 / 16 \\
Batch size & 8 \\
Learning rate & $5 \times 10^{-4}$ (constant) \\
Training steps & 50{,}000 \\
Aux.\ loss weight $\lambda_0$ & 0.01 \\
Aux.\ loss decay & $\exp(-t / 50{,}000)$ \\
BEV grid resolution & $64 \times 64$ \\
Focal loss $\alpha$ / $\gamma$ & 0.25 / 2.0 \\
Depth loss & Smooth-L1 \\
Aux.\ head parameters & 1.2M \\
Hardware & NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (98\,GB) \\
Wall time per run & $\sim$12.9 hours \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Evaluation protocol.} Each condition uses 3+ seeds (Depth uses 4; Cutoff@15K, Cutoff@25K, and Reverse Cutoff use 2; Cutoff@45K uses 1) with 50 rollouts per task per seed (500 episodes per seed). A collision is registered when the L1 displacement of the obstacle's \texttt{body\_xpos} exceeds $10^{-3}$. Bootstrap 95\% CIs are computed via 10{,}000 resamples (percentile method).

\textbf{Statistical tests.} Pairwise comparisons against Vanilla use two-tailed paired $t$-tests on per-seed mean CARs with Cohen's $d$ for effect size. With $n{=}3$ seeds, statistical power is limited; we report $p$-values and effect sizes for transparency.
\textbf{CKA analysis.} Centered Kernel Alignment~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} measures representational similarity between fine-tuned and pretrained encoder features. We extract patch token activations from the final layer of DINOv2 and SigLIP for 1{,}000 validation images, compute linear CKA per layer, and report the mean across layers. Higher CKA indicates better preservation of pretrained representations.

\textbf{Cutoff implementation.} As described in Section~\ref{sec:ablation}, setting $\lambda(t){=}0$ does not disable the auxiliary head's forward pass; the post-cutoff phase operates in Detach mode with BatchNorm statistics continuing to update (see the Cutoff description therein for details).

\section{Extended Analysis}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/gradient_analysis_combined.pdf}
\caption{Gradient dynamics during VLA fine-tuning (preliminary analysis using v1 codebase checkpoint; seed 42, 200 batches at 50K checkpoint). (a)~Action loss gradient norms per encoder layer: Vanilla, Full OCC, and Random OCC are comparable, while Detach shows substantially reduced norms. (b)~Auxiliary loss gradient norms in the shared encoder (Detach excluded; no auxiliary gradients by design). (c)~Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity per layer (near zero for both Full OCC and Random OCC, confirming orthogonality). (d)~Gradient norm ratio (Detach / Vanilla) per layer. V2 validation is ongoing; directional conclusions should be interpreted with caution.}
\label{fig:gradient_analysis}
\end{figure}

The Detach+LN ablation replaces BatchNorm2d with LayerNorm in the auxiliary head while retaining \texttt{stop\_gradient}. Results (3 seeds) are reported in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} and analyzed in Section~\ref{sec:why_gradient}. Detach+LN (CAR 0.765) approaches the Vanilla baseline, consistent with the interaction pattern: removing gradient flow mitigates safety degradation regardless of normalization choice. The interaction pattern (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}) indicates that gradient flow is associated with lower safety under both BN and LN, with BN amplifying the deficit.

\textbf{Per-layer gradient analysis.} Figure~\ref{fig:per_layer_combined} decomposes auxiliary gradient flow into two dimensions: amplitude (top row) and direction (bottom row). The amplitude heatmap (panels a--b) shows that Vanilla, Full OCC, and Random OCC maintain comparable action gradient norms across all layers (warm colors), while Detach shows substantially reduced norms across both encoders (dark). These amplitude results were generated from v1 codebase checkpoints; preliminary v2 analysis suggests the Detach gradient reduction may not reproduce at the same magnitude, and validation with the current codebase is pending. The directional analysis (panels c--d) reveals encoder-specific alignment: DINOv2 conflict rates hover near 50\% across all layers while SigLIP late layers (blocks 21--24) drop to 19--25\%, which indicates cooperative gradient alignment in semantically rich layers. Both patterns are identical for Full OCC and Random OCC, which confirms they arise from architectural structure rather than label content. The aggregate conflict rates (Full OCC 44.1\%, Random OCC 43.9\%) fall below the 50\% expected for random vectors because they reflect this mixture of two encoder-specific patterns.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/per_layer_combined.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer gradient analysis decomposed into amplitude and direction (v1 codebase checkpoint, seed 42, 200 batches; v2 validation pending). \textbf{Top (a--b):} Action gradient norm heatmap (log scale). Gradient-flow conditions (Vanilla, Full OCC, Random OCC) maintain comparable norms; Detach shows substantially reduced norms across all layers. \textbf{Bottom (c--d):} Gradient conflict rate between action and auxiliary losses. DINOv2 maintains $\sim$50\% conflict (near-orthogonal), while SigLIP late layers drop to 19--25\% (cooperative alignment). Both amplitude and directional patterns are identical for Full OCC and Random OCC, which confirms architectural rather than label-dependent structure. Directional analysis (c--d) is confirmed in v2; amplitude analysis (a--b) is pending v2 validation.}
\label{fig:per_layer_combined}
\end{figure}

\label{app:temporal}
\textbf{Dose-response: gradient flow duration vs.\ CAR (v1 data).} Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response} plots CAR against the number of OCC gradient flow steps for the cutoff series (15K--45K, plus Detach at 0K and Full OCC at 50K; all v1 code). Cutoff@15K (2 seeds, CAR 0.782) clusters near Vanilla (0.780), while Cutoff@25K (2 seeds, 0.724) falls substantially below. Multi-seed conditions (Cutoff@35K and Full OCC) also cluster near the Vanilla baseline. Reverse Cutoff (late 15K) falls below the early-cutoff counterpart at the same gradient flow duration, consistent with the continuity hypothesis. The pattern suggests intermediate cutoff $\approx$ Vanilla rather than a non-monotonic dose-response; the earlier single-seed Cutoff@15K result (0.804) was seed noise.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Dose-response: CAR vs.\ OCC gradient flow duration (v1 codebase, all conditions). Filled circles: early-cutoff conditions with all 3 seeds completed; open circles: conditions with $\leq$2 seeds (counts annotated as $n$). Diamond ($\diamond$): Reverse Cutoff (gradient flow restricted to the last 15K steps). Dashed line: Vanilla 3-seed mean (v1); shaded band: $\pm\,$1\,SE. The ``+0.2\%'' annotation marks the Cutoff@15K--Vanilla gap. Intermediate cutoff conditions cluster near Vanilla, while Cutoff@25K and Reverse Cutoff fall substantially below.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\section{Additional Observations}
\label{app:additional}

\textbf{Per-task concentration.} Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla; overall CAR is dominated by three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6; Section~\ref{app:per_task}). Under the v1 codebase, Detach showed the lowest CAR on these hard tasks (T3: 0.11, T5: 0.05). However, this v1-specific pattern does not replicate under the controlled v2 factorial design (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), where detaching gradient flow consistently preserves safety (Detach OCC CAR 0.767 vs.\ Vanilla 0.745); the v1 per-task pattern may reflect implementation-specific artifacts rather than a generalizable effect of gradient detachment.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla (v1 data).} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039, identical to Vanilla (v1, 0.780), despite providing per-pixel geometric information. Three label types (structured BEV, random binary, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained.

\textbf{Temporal dynamics (preliminary, v1 data).} Cutoff@35K (3 seeds) achieves CAR 0.779, statistically indistinguishable from Vanilla (v1, $\Delta = -$0.1\,pp), which confirms that 35K steps of gradient flow fully protects collision avoidance. Surprisingly, Cutoff@25K (0.724, 2 seeds) and Reverse Cutoff (0.718, 2 seeds) fall substantially \emph{below} Vanilla. The most parsimonious explanation: setting the loss weight to zero retains the auxiliary head forward pass, so the post-cutoff period operates in Detach mode; BatchNorm interference acts on representations shaped by gradient flow, without gradient compensation. This is not withdrawal from gradient flow \emph{per se}, but transition into an actively harmful regime. Third seeds are in progress; extended analysis in Section~\ref{app:temporal}.

\section{Codebase Sensitivity (v1 vs.\ v2)}
\label{app:codebase}

The five core factorial conditions were retrained under a revised codebase (v2) that fixed a batch-index resume bug affecting cutoff timing and restructured the auxiliary head pipeline (module initialization order, data loading). All model architectures, hyperparameters, LoRA configuration, and evaluation protocols are identical between v1 and v2. The Vanilla baseline (no auxiliary head) is unaffected by these changes, since the revision touches only auxiliary-head code paths.

\textbf{Behavioral shift.} Under v1, Full OCC achieved a mean CAR of 0.797 (3 seeds, per-task Table~\ref{tab:per_task}); under v2, the same condition yields 0.725 $\pm$ 0.064 (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Same-seed comparisons show shifts of up to 8.6\,pp, while Vanilla remains stable across codebases ($<$1.5\,pp). This sensitivity is confined to conditions with auxiliary heads and is itself informative: it indicates that auxiliary head training dynamics are fragile with respect to implementation details that would not normally be considered hyperparameters.

\textbf{Gradient analysis shift.} The v1 codebase exhibited a Detach gradient suppression artifact: Detach action-loss gradient norms were only $\sim$4\% of Vanilla norms across all encoder layers. In v2, this suppression disappears entirely (Detach/Vanilla ratio $\approx$ 1.29), indicating that the v1 pattern was a code-path artifact rather than a genuine effect of gradient detachment. The gradient conflict rate also shifted from $\sim$44\% (v1) to $\sim$50\% (v2), with v2 values closer to the theoretical expectation for orthogonal vectors.

\textbf{Interaction pattern shift.} Under v1, the normalization $\times$ gradient flow interaction appeared as a crossover pattern ($p{=}0.025$); under v2, the pattern is ordinal (both lines slope downward without crossing). The crossover pattern in v1 is likely attributable to the Detach gradient suppression artifact described above, which differentially affected BN and LN conditions. All main-text analyses use v2 data exclusively; v1 results are retained in the appendix for completeness and to document the sensitivity.

\textbf{Implication.} The codebase sensitivity reinforces our central finding: auxiliary spatial heads introduce optimization fragility that implementation details can amplify or attenuate. This fragility is a further argument against adding auxiliary spatial heads for safety purposes, since the safety outcome depends on implementation choices that are difficult to specify, reproduce, or audit.

\section{Statistical Power Analysis}
\label{app:stat_power}

The $2{\times}2$ factorial design prioritizes breadth (12 conditions, 5 factors) over per-cell statistical power. For the BN row, the observed effect size is large (Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$), but with $n{=}3$ vs.\ $n{=}4$ seeds per cell, post-hoc power is approximately 0.3--0.4~\cite{cohen1988statistical}, meaning there is a 60--70\% probability of failing to detect a true effect of this magnitude at $\alpha{=}0.05$. We report this limitation transparently.

However, the study's evidential strength does not rest on any single pairwise comparison. The consistent gradient flow effect---both BN and LN rows show lower CAR under gradient flow ON than OFF---provides the primary evidence for the ordinal interaction pattern, complemented by the large observed effect size (Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$) in the fully powered BN row. Seed expansion ($n{=}5$ per factorial cell) is underway to increase per-cell power.

\section{Limitations}
\label{app:limitations}

Our study uses a single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT with LoRA rank 32); full fine-tuning may alter the interaction between auxiliary heads and encoder optimization because all parameters update simultaneously, potentially changing the gradient flow dynamics we observe. All experiments are conducted in MuJoCo simulation (SafeLIBERO Level~I, 10 tasks); real-world deployment introduces additional sources of distributional shift that could interact differently with auxiliary head training dynamics. The benchmark contains only static obstacles; dynamic obstacle scenarios may induce time-varying distributions where auxiliary head dynamics behave differently. Our collision metric is binary (L1 displacement threshold $10^{-3}$), which does not capture collision severity or near-miss behavior; a continuous safety metric might reveal finer-grained differences between conditions. CKA measures linear representational similarity, which may miss nonlinear structure relevant to safety behavior. The interaction pattern is based on $n{=}1$--$4$ seeds per cell; larger-scale replication would further strengthen conclusions. Cutoff conditions have 1--3 seeds, limiting temporal conclusions to preliminary observations.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes TSR and CAR across conditions (50 rollouts $\times$ 10 tasks per seed). $\Delta$CAR is computed against the matched-codebase Vanilla baseline (v2: $0.745 \pm 0.024$, 3 seeds, for factorial conditions above the midrule; v1: 0.780 for supplementary conditions below). Random OCC ($^\star$) uses v1 code but is included above the midrule as a factorial control; its $\Delta$CAR is omitted due to the cross-codebase baseline mismatch. Statistical comparisons use paired $t$-tests and Cohen's $d$ against the Vanilla baseline.

\textbf{Interaction pattern between gradient flow and normalization.} The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) show a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization type; whether normalization modulates the magnitude requires additional LN seeds to confirm (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}). Full OCC with BN (gradient flow ON, BatchNorm) is the worst-performing factorial cell (CAR 0.725, $\Delta{=}-2.0$\,pp vs.\ Vanilla, $-$4.2\,pp vs.\ Detach OCC within the BN factorial row) and the most unstable ($\sigma{=}0.064$, more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells, though this variance estimate is itself uncertain at $n{=}3$). Full OCC+LN (gradient flow ON, LayerNorm) also degrades CAR below Vanilla (0.740, $\Delta{=}-0.5$\,pp). Detaching gradient flow recovers safety: Detach OCC (0.767, $\Delta{=}+2.2$\,pp) matches or slightly exceeds the Vanilla baseline (0.745); Detach+LN$^\star$ (0.765) shows a comparable level under v1 code. These results are consistent with the hypothesis that spatial label content does not detectably affect outcomes under the conditions tested: Full OCC (ground-truth occupancy), Random OCC (random binary labels), and Depth (egocentric depth maps) all fall within cross-seed variance ($p > 0.4$, observed under v1 codebase for Random OCC and Depth).

\textbf{BN + gradient flow destabilizes training.} Full OCC (BN) exhibits the highest variance of any factorial cell ($\sigma{=}0.064$, more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells): individual seeds range from 0.776 (near Vanilla) to 0.654 ($\Delta{=}-9.1$\,pp), indicating a volatile optimization landscape under the BN + gradient flow combination.


